    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d263">

                <pb facs="0371" n="303" sameAs="frus:frus1914-20v01/pg303"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">763.72112/1852½: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-10-15T16:00:00+00:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 15, 1915—4 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [Received 10:12 p. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3025. . . .</p>
                <p>The Government and public opinion here are in about the same mood that Northern
                    opinion and Lincoln’s administration were in the week after Bull Run or after
                    Lee crossed the Potomac on his way to Gettysburg. The Balkan situation and the
                    Dardanelles tragedy threaten a political upheaval and the public feeling is far
                    more tense than at any [previous time]. Many of our best friends here fear that
                    it is an unfortunate moment for our long note to be presented<anchor n="91"
                        xml:id="d263-f1"/> while the present crisis lasts. Bryce has this feeling
                    strongly. Northcliffe who has always been most friendly and helpful in his
                    newspapers is greatly excited lest a [violent] feeling be provoked not so much
                    by the note itself as by its coming now. I send you these strong convictions by
                    these men and many more like them for whatever they may be worth and without
                    recommendation of my own. But it is certain that the note will receive no
                    serious attention by the Government till the present tension is relaxed, and its
                    presentation at this moment is likely to result in a public reception that may
                    tend to defeat its purpose [<hi rendition="#italic">object</hi>].</p>
                <closer>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Am[erican] Ambassador</hi>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
